WEBVTT

00:00:04.994 --> 00:00:14.906
A report can use inline JSON, a CSV file or a REST API. First test the data source, then bind its fields so the report uses current values.

00:00:19.164 --> 00:00:24.927
Open a blank report and choose Data. The side panel holds both datasets and report parameters.

00:00:27.873 --> 00:00:35.672
Add a parameter called userId with sample value 1. The host application can provide a different value each time it renders the report.

00:00:40.106 --> 00:00:49.764
Create a REST dataset and add the parameter to the URL. The server makes the request; API credentials belong in server secrets, not in the report file.

00:00:52.574 --> 00:00:59.596
Preview data sends this sample request. Check the returned fields and confirm the response has the shape the report expects.

00:01:04.132 --> 00:01:11.229
Save the dataset, then drag the returned name field onto the page. It becomes a bound value rather than fixed text.

00:01:18.303 --> 00:01:26.009
Add a CSV dataset and preview its rows. The first row supplies the field names, which you can then bind like API fields.

00:01:30.702 --> 00:01:41.447
Database sources use a named PostgreSQL or MySQL connection configured by the host on the server. The report keeps a connection name and parameterized SQL, not a password.

00:01:47.592 --> 00:01:56.783
Run Preview to check the report using the sample API response. Use the host application to control which record and credentials are supplied at render time.

00:01:58.191 --> 00:02:06.726
For production, validate the API response shape and configure credentials on the server. Keep tenant and record access in your host application.
